// Regression for the 6y4y design decision: F1005 gates only NON-NULLABLE missing
// fields. A nullable (`T?`) field may be omitted from a full literal — it
// defaults to its natural absent value, null. `make` below omits the nullable
// `note`, so this file would FAIL TO COMPILE if the gate wrongly required
// nullable fields — that is the mutation guard. (The negative case — a missing
// NON-nullable field → F1005 — lives in tests/err_missing_struct_field_test.av.)
//
// ONE CLAIM PER `then` (uzs9.2). This spec used to assert
// `r.id == 7 && r.count == 3 && r.note == null` in a single arm. It is the
// known flaky spec under parallel shards — hit twice on byte-identical PRs
// (#671, #673) — and has since survived 175 whole-suite repro iterations, a
// full runtime thread-safety audit, and 19.2M dense concurrent probes under
// AVRA_VERIFY_RC=1 without reproducing. Four investigations, no repro.
//
// So the realistic path is not to catch it on demand but to make the NEXT
// occurrence informative, and the conjunction destroyed exactly the
// information that would settle it: a failing `then` reports only its name, so
// both historical hits said nothing beyond "the conjunction was false".
// Split, the arm that fails IS the diagnosis:
//
//   `note` alone      → the omitted-nullable null-init store, or slot reuse
//   `id` or `count`   → the whole Rec is freed or clobbered (RC placement)
//
// Every claim is still asserted — nothing is weakened, only attributed.
//
// Each arm builds its OWN Rec rather than sharing one from the enclosing
// `given`. That is the suite's established shape, and it is forced here: a
// `let` in a `given` body does not reach its `then` arms (t-4nly — a real bug,
// filed, and the emitter's own docstring claims the opposite). It is also the
// better probe for a rare concurrent fault: three independent allocations
// sample more interleavings than one shared allocation read three times.
//
// The general fix — a failing `then` naming which conjunct of a `&&` chain was
// false, for every spec in the suite — is t-kdyj.5.
type Rec = { id: int, note: string?, count: int }

/// A nullable field (`note`) omitted; every non-nullable field supplied.
fn make() -> Rec { Rec { id: 7, count: 3 } }

spec "F1005: a nullable field may be omitted from a struct literal (6y4y)" {
    then "the omitted nullable field defaults to null" {
        let r = make()
        r.note == null
    }
    then "the supplied `id` survives the omission" {
        let r = make()
        r.id == 7
    }
    then "the supplied `count` survives the omission" {
        let r = make()
        r.count == 3
    }
    then "a full literal round-trips the nullable field" {
        let full = Rec { id: 1, note: "hi", count: 2 }
        full.note == "hi"
    }
    then "a full literal keeps its non-nullable fields" {
        let full = Rec { id: 1, note: "hi", count: 2 }
        full.id == 1
    }
}
